 Use :practice to learn the editing basics. This documentation
 is about the other UI features, the ones that don't convert
 well into buffer exercises.

        Press j until you reach the first section. To
        leave, type :q! and press Enter.



=================================================================
=                  1 OPENING, SAVING, QUITTING                  =
=================================================================

 Commands start with : and run when you press Enter. Tab
 completes their names and arguments, and Up/Down go through
 the ones you ran before.

  :              start a command
  :o PATH        open a file (also :e and :edit)
  :w             write the file; :w PATH writes it to PATH
  :q             quit; refused when there are unsaved changes
  :q!            quit and throw the changes away
  :wq  or  :x    write, then quit
  :wa  :qa       write all files, quit all windows

 This tutor is a buffer without a file. Try :w /tmp/tutor.txt,
 then :q, and open it again with vx /tmp/tutor.txt.

 vx alone, or vx FOLDER (which also makes FOLDER the working
 directory), opens on the dashboard (this fork): the project's
 branch, remote, uncommitted changes and languages in a git
 repository, and a key for each way to start. Opening a file
 from anywhere replaces it; every other key works as usual.

  :dashboard     show the dashboard again
                 On the dashboard:
  i              start typing in the empty buffer under it
  Esc            the empty buffer, in normal mode
  f  e  l  q     file picker, file tree, git history, quit
  t              the worktrees, when the repository has others

 Try it: :dashboard, then Esc. To start without it, set
 dashboard = false under [editor] in your config. The vortex
 behind it grows from the center and keeps spinning; set
 dashboard-background to change that: "grow" (grows, then
 stops), "drain" (starts full, then flows away), "settle"
 (spins, then slows to a stop), "on" (full and spinning from
 the start), "static" (still) or "off". To leave out its title
 and credit line, dashboard-title = false.



=================================================================
=                          2 BUFFERS                            =
=================================================================

 Every file you open stays open in a buffer, even when another
 one is on screen.

  gn  gp         next and previous buffer
  gm             the buffer you last changed
  space b        pick a buffer from a list
  :bc            close the buffer (:bc! when it has changes)
  :new           a new empty buffer

 Try it: :o README.md (or any file), then ga twice: ga goes back
 to where you came from, and back again (section 5).



=================================================================
=                          3 PICKERS                            =
=================================================================

 A picker is a list with a fuzzy filter: type part of a name
 and the list narrows. Up/Down or C-p/C-n move, Enter opens.

  space f        files of the project (the git repository)
  space F        files of the current directory
  space .        files of the current file's directory
  space /        search the text of every file
  space '        open the last picker again, where you left it
  space ?        every command, with its keys; the ones you
                 ran this session come first

 The space menu groups its keys under labels: files, search,
 git, LSP... The fork's own lists (the commit log, the changes,
 the worktrees, the bookmarks, the file tree) start in normal
 mode, where letters are keys: s filters, ? lists their keys.

 In a picker:

  C-s  C-v       open in a horizontal or vertical split
  C-t            hide or show the preview
  Esc            close

 space ? is the way to find anything this tutor leaves out:
 type a few letters of what you want to do.



=================================================================
=                    4 THE FILE TREE (FORK)                     =
=================================================================

  space e        a tree of the project
  space E        the same, opened on the current file

 The tree starts in normal mode, where letters are keys:

  j  k           move
  l  h           open a directory or a file, close or go up
  s              filter: type to search the whole tree
  a              create a file (end the name in / for a folder)
  r              rename or move (the name may contain /)
  x, then p      cut, then paste into the selected directory
  d, then y      delete
  H              show or hide the files git ignores
  ?              list the keys

 Files changed in git are colored and marked M, A, U, R or !.



=================================================================
=               5 HISTORY AND BOOKMARKS (FORK)                  =
=================================================================

 History is automatic: each teleport remembers where it left.
 Teleports are gd and the other goto keys, a search (/ ? n N),
 gg, ge, :12, a result picked in a picker, another buffer, and
 another screen (the dashboard, a diff, the debug panel). Moving
 through the text around the cursor (hjkl w b f } % gt...) never
 counts, so history stays short enough to trust.

  ga             back to where the last jump left, and again to
                 return: ga ga is where you were
  gA             one step further back each time; then A goes on
                 back, a forward, Esc stops, any other key stops
                 and does what it always does

 After a walk with gA, ga returns to where the walk started.
 Jumping somewhere new from the middle of a walk forgets the
 steps ahead of it, like an edit after u forgets the redo.

 Try it: in code with a language server, gd twice into other
 files (or :o two files of your own), then gAAA, a, and ga.

 Bookmarks are places you set yourself and keep coming back to:
 a working set across files, in the order you set them.

  C-s            set a bookmark on the cursor's line, or remove
                 the one there (with several cursors, on each
                 cursor's line)
  C-i  (Tab)     the next bookmark, in the order they were set;
                 past the last, the first again
  C-o            the previous bookmark
  space j        the bookmarks in a list: Enter goes to one,
                 C-x removes it

 The gutter shows each bookmark's number, 1 to 9, then ◆. They
 move with the text when lines come and go above them, and go
 with their line when it is deleted. They last until Vortex
 quits. Removing one renumbers the ones after it.

 Try it: C-s on three lines in two files, then C-i C-i C-i and
 C-o to go round them; then space j, C-x on number 2, and see
 3 become 2 in the gutter. C-i records a jump too: ga is back.



=================================================================
=                       6 GOING PLACES                          =
=================================================================

  12G  or  :12   line 12
  12g|           column 12 of this line
  g.             the last change
  gw             a two-letter label on every word: type a label
                 to jump there, Esc to cancel
  gt  gc  gb     the top, the middle, the bottom of the screen
  gf             the file whose path is under the cursor
  gj  gk         down and up a line of the file, when long lines
                 wrap and j and k move by screen lines

 [ and ] go to the previous and the next thing of a kind:

  ]p  [p         paragraph       ]t  [t   class or type
  ]a  [a         parameter       ]c  [c   comment
  ]T  [T         test            ]e  [e   entry (key: value)
  ]x  [x         XML/HTML tag    ]g  [g   git change
  ]f  [f         function        ]d  [d   diagnostic
  ]D  [D         the last, the first diagnostic

 Most of them need tree-sitter to know the language.



=================================================================
=                     7 SCROLLING THE VIEW                      =
=================================================================

  C-d  C-u       half a page down and up, with the cursor
  C-f  C-b       a page down and up

 z changes the view without moving the cursor:

  zz             this line to the middle of the screen
  zm             the cursor to the middle, sideways, when long
                 lines don't wrap
  zt  zb         this line to the top, to the bottom
  zj  zk         scroll a line down, up

 Z is the same, but stays until Esc, so Zjjj scrolls on.



=================================================================
=                          8 SPLITS                             =
=================================================================

 The screen can show several buffers, or one buffer twice. C-w
 (or space w) starts a window command:

  C-w v  C-w s   split vertically, horizontally
  C-w h j k l    go to the split on the left, below, ...
  C-w w          go to the next split
  C-w H J K L    swap this split with the one on the left, ...
  C-w t          turn vertical splits horizontal, and back
  C-w q          close this split
  C-w o          close all the others
  C-w n v        a new split with an empty buffer (C-w n s:
                 a horizontal one)
  C-w f  C-w F   open the file under the cursor in a split, a
                 vertical split

  :vs PATH       open a file in a vertical split (:hs for
                 horizontal)

 Try it: C-w v, scroll the new split, C-w h, then C-w o.



=================================================================
=                   9 THE SYSTEM CLIPBOARD                      =
=================================================================

 y and p use Vortex's own register. Space puts the system
 clipboard in their place:

  space y        yank to the clipboard
  space Y        yank only the primary selection
  space p  P     paste the clipboard after, before
  space R        replace the selection with the clipboard

 The clipboard is also the register +, so "+y works too. The
 register _ throws away what goes into it: "_d deletes and
 keeps the yank.



=================================================================
=                     10 LANGUAGE SERVERS                       =
=================================================================

 With a language server for the language (see vx --health),
 Vortex understands the code:

  gd  gD         go to the definition, the declaration
  gy  gi         the type, the implementation
  gr             pick a reference
  space k        documentation of the name under the cursor
  space r        rename it everywhere
  space h        select its references in this file
  space a        code actions (fixes, imports, ...)
  space s  S     pick a symbol of the file, of the workspace
  space d  D     pick a diagnostic of the file, of the workspace
  ]d  [d         next and previous diagnostic
  =              format the selection (:format for the file)

 In insert mode:

  C-x            complete the word, with the server's
                 suggestions

 :lsp-restart restarts the servers, and :log-open shows what
 went wrong.



=================================================================
=                            11 GIT                             =
=================================================================

 The gutter marks the lines changed since the last commit.

  ]g  [g         next and previous change; ]G  [G the last, first
  :diffget       put the change under the cursor back as it was
  space g        pick a changed file, staged or not; C-g there
                 shows its diff
  space =        this file next to its last commit, side by side
                 (fork): n N go from change to change, q closes;
                 s u r stage, unstage and revert the change, and
                 S U R the whole file. A revert is an edit of the
                 file: u in its buffer brings it back
  space l        the commit log (fork): Enter lists the changes
                 of a commit, one row per change under a line
                 per file, marked + adds, - removes, -+ both,
                 ++ new file, -- deleted file. w b jump to the
                 next and previous file, Enter on a change shows
                 its diff there; v selects several commits. Its
                 first row is what you haven't committed, in the
                 same list, also marked S staged, M not staged,
                 • not saved yet. It follows your edits while
                 it's open, even from another program. In its
                 diffs, n N go on to the next file. In its
                 filter, a hash (4 hex digits or more) finds
                 the commit
  space B        who changed each line, when, and in which
                 commit (fork)
  space L        the history of the cursor's line, or of the
                 selected lines (fork, :line-history): only the
                 commits that changed them, the one that added
                 them last, marked (added). Enter shows the diff
                 right at the change, o the whole commit. % then
                 space L is the history of the whole file
  :wt            the repository's worktrees (fork, :worktrees):
                 the current one first, then the most recently
                 changed, each with its uncommitted changes and
                 unpushed commits; Enter goes to one, like :cd
  :watch         follow an agent at work (fork, vx --watch, w
                 on the dashboard): each change it makes is
                 opened, jumped to and flashed, and marked ┃
                 in the gutter. It is a mode, with a bar at the
                 bottom: its state, the change, and its keys.
                 Keys don't stop it by accident: v looks around
                 with your own cursor (v again follows), n N go
                 through the changes in the order they happened
                 (n to the newest follows again), c lists them,
                 Esc leaves it to edit where you are
  space i        yank the diagnostic under the cursor; with
                 none, and blame on, the line's commit hash

 A file another program changed is reloaded when you come back
 to it, unless you changed it too: then :reload takes the new
 version from the disk, and :write! keeps yours (fork).



=================================================================
=                       12 THE SHELL                            =
=================================================================

 :practice has | (pipe through a command). There are more:

  !              insert the output of a command before the
                 selection
  A-!            append it after the selection
  A-|            pipe the selection to a command, ignore output
  $              keep the selections for which the command
                 succeeds
  :sh CMD        run a command and show its output
  C-z            suspend Vortex (fg in the shell to come back)



=================================================================
=                     13 BACK IN TIME                           =
=================================================================

 u and U go one change at a time. To go by time:

  A-u  A-U       back and forward through every state in the
                 order it happened, undone branches included
  :earlier 10m   the file as it was ten minutes ago
  :later 5m      and forward again

 In insert mode:

  C-s            a checkpoint: u undoes only what you typed after
                 it



