=================================================================
=                        15 THE DEBUGGER                        =
=================================================================

 With a debug adapter for the language (see vx --health; Vortex
 installs none), the debugger is a mode: it has its own keys and
 a bar of three rows above the message area, like a video
 player. The first row says what the program does (DEBUG paused,
 playing...) and has the transport: |< << > >> >|. The second is
 the timeline of the run, which you can click to go back to a
 moment. The third lists the keys. A panel opens below your
 splits while the program runs: its variables, your watches, the
 call stack, the breakpoints and a console.

  space G        debug mode: the debugger as a video player
  Esc            leave debug mode; the program keeps running
  ?              in debug mode, every key, in groups
  vx --debug PROGRAM
                 start Vortex in debug mode, launching PROGRAM

 In debug mode:

  s              start: pick how to launch the program
  Space          play or pause the program, line by line
  l  h           the next line, the previous line (back in time)
  L  H           the next breakpoint, the previous one
  b              add or remove a breakpoint on this line
  B              its type: any thread, the main thread only,
                 or the other threads only
  C  M           a condition for the breakpoint; a log message
                 for it instead of a stop
  v              view mode: look around with your own cursor
                 while the program goes on; v again follows it
  x  w           evaluate an expression; watch one
  S              end the session

 The view follows the program: the line it stands at stays in
 the middle of the screen, and the movement keys wait for v
 (the bar says so). Breakpoints are set only in debug mode, and
 only there they are in the gutter.

 In the panel, j and k move, Tab goes back to the editor, 1 to 5
 change section, Enter opens a value (or jumps to a frame or
 breakpoint), c changes it, a watches it, s searches. The keys
 the panel doesn't use, like Space and L, are debug mode's.
 Ctrl-g gives the keys to the panel from anywhere, and Esc goes
 back to the editor; Esc again in normal mode hides the panel.

 Where the adapter reads memory, a value that lives in memory
 has a ▦ after it, and M shows its bytes in the hex view, in a
 split, at its address. They are read again at every stop, and
 the bytes the program changed since the stop before stand out.
 i overwrites them while the program is paused (where the
 adapter writes memory), :w sends them, and u writes the old
 ones back. A byte that can't be read shows ??.

  M              in the panel, the memory of the value
  m              the same, in debug mode
  :debug-memory  the memory of an expression, or at 0x7ffe…



=================================================================
=                       16 SPECTATOR MODE                       =
=================================================================

 Watch the program like a video: it plays line by line at a
 speed you choose, following one thread, and every stop is kept
 so you can scrub back to what you missed. It is what debug mode
 does when you press Space.

 When the session starts, Vortex puts a hidden breakpoint on
 every line of your code (the bar shows them going in), so the
 program stops at the first line of yours that runs. Playing
 continues it to its next line, wherever that is; the other
 threads run on. L runs at full speed to your next breakpoint.
 e to lib or std hides them in the libraries too: a work in
 progress, and absurdly slow. With engine = "stepping" under
 [editor.debugger], playback steps instead.

 In debug mode:

  Space          play or pause; l makes one step
  k  j           faster, slower: 0.5 to 25 lines a second
  e              what it steps into: own code (the default),
                 lib (libraries too), std (the runtime too)
  f              fast forward until a watch (or a variable you
                 marked with * in the panel) changes, and show
                 old -> new
  r              run, or play, to the cursor's line
  < >            follow the previous, the next thread
  t  F           pick a thread, a stack frame
  z              freeze the followed thread: it never runs
                 until z again; a: only it runs
  h  H           back through the recorded stops, or to the
                 previous breakpoint, read-only, under REWIND;
                 l and L go forward, p comes back to live
  c              what each stop records: depth, size, history
  X              clear the time labels

 The timeline is the second row of the bar: denser cells are
 where the thread ran most, marks show breakpoints, pauses and
 switches of thread, and a click on it goes to that moment. A
 step that doesn't come back shows "blocked in" and a timer;
 Space pauses it where it waits. After each step the line it
 left shows how long it took, greyed at its end, so slow lines
 stand out.



=================================================================
=                     17 THE TERMINAL PANEL                     =
=================================================================

 A shell runs in a panel below your splits. It keeps running
 while the panel is hidden, and SH in the statusline says what
 it does: yellow while a command runs, then green or red for
 how it ended, until you look at the panel again.

  C-t            show the panel and type in it; C-t again goes
                 back to the editor, with the panel still shown
  Esc            in normal mode, hide the panel
  :sh CMD        run CMD in the panel's shell, as if typed
  :! CMD         run CMD and show its output in a popup

 In the panel, vx FILE opens the file in this editor instead of
 starting another Vortex, and git commit edits its message here:
 :wq when done, and you are back in the shell. exit closes the
 panel; C-t starts a new shell.

 In insert mode:

  C-t            the panel, as in normal mode



=================================================================
=                        18 THE HEX VIEW                        =
=================================================================

 A binary file (an image, an executable...) opens in the hex
 view: offsets, the bytes in hex, and the same bytes as ASCII.
 It reads only what it shows, so even a huge file opens at once.
 :hex shows any file this way, and back as text.

  h l  j k       a byte, a row
  w b            the next, the previous word of 4 bytes
  gg  ge         the first, the last byte
  Tab            go between the hex and the ASCII column
  ~              show bits instead of hex digits, and back
  G              go to an offset: 0x1a4 in hex, 420 in decimal
                 (:goto 0x1a4 does the same)
  / ?  n N       search hex bytes (de ad be ef) or "text"
  v  y           select bytes, yank them as hex text

 It is read-only: typing only reminds you that i overwrites.
 After i, typing overwrites the byte under the cursor, a hex
 digit, a bit or a character at a time, depending on the column
 (the file never grows or shrinks), until Esc. u undoes, and :w
 writes back only the bytes that changed.



=================================================================
=                      19 MORE EDITING KEYS                     =
=================================================================

 These keys change text or selections too, but :practice has no
 exercise for them: in its short texts they save no keys over
 what it teaches. They are still worth knowing.

  X              extend to whole lines; unlike x, it never adds
                 the next line
  A-x            shrink to the whole lines inside the selection
  A-;            flip the selection: the cursor goes to the
                 other end
  A-:            make every selection point forward
  A--            merge the selections into one
  A-_            merge the selections that touch
  A-i            shrink the selection to a child node, back
                 down after A-o
  A-I            select every child of the selected node
  A-e  A-b       go to the end, the start of the parent node
  space A-c      comment with line comments, even where C-c
                 would pick a block comment
  ]space [space  add an empty line below, above, and stay in
                 normal mode

 In insert mode:

  C-u  C-k       delete to the start, the end of the line
  A-d            delete the word after the cursor (also A-Del)
  Backspace  C-h  delete the character before the cursor
  Del  C-d       delete the character under the cursor
  Tab            indent; after text, jump past the end of the
                 bracket or string the cursor is in (smart-tab)
  S-Tab          a real tab, whatever the indentation style
  C-j            a new line, like Enter
  Home  End      the start, the end of the line
  PageUp         scroll a page up; PageDown, a page down



